tellraw @a {"text": "Technology: Archimedes' screw", "color": "gold", "bold": true}
tellraw @a {"text": "Inclined screw in a casing lifting water from a pool to a channel.", "color": "white"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Hellenistic Greece / Ptolemaic Egypt, 3rd century BCE", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "A helical blade inside an inclined tube; turning it lifts water in pockets up the spiral.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Used to drain mines and ships' bilges and to irrigate the Nile Delta, where Diodorus says", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Archimedes saw it (or devised it) in Egypt.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "The screw is inclined at about 30 to 45 degrees with its lower end in the water. Each turn", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "traps a pocket of water between the blade and the casing and carries it one pitch higher.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Workers turned it by treading on the outside of the casing.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "See it in context: /function ac:egypt/alexandria", "color": "aqua"}
